// Reactivity S0 — dedupe uses the language's `==`, which is STRUCTURAL for heap values (lists, maps).
// A dedupe signal holding a list suppresses a `set` to a structurally-equal list ([1, 2, 3] again) and
// fires only when the contents actually differ. So the effect prints the length on creation (3) and
// once more when the list changes (4) — never for the equal re-set.
// expect: stdout "len=3"
// expect: stdout "len=4"
// expect: exit 0
use std.reactive.{signal, effect}
items = signal([1, 2, 3], true)
effect(fn() {
    echo "len=${items.get().len()}"
})
items.set([1, 2, 3])      // structurally equal → suppressed
items.set([1, 2, 3, 4])   // different contents → rerun
items.set([1, 2, 3, 4])   // equal again → suppressed
